{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-41787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/assets/7a3c82568cfaf845708a9dcee79a3ac2e0e9d2131776fd1c7daa1c598473f3b3.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=10$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/state.json" "file_sha256" "6001777ea447cce3ae7f8cc6ab0ea89cda6289dae88ba9083f4a5a10f86ed8de"} {"question" "q-41787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(10,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-5\\sqrt{3},-5).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/state.json" "file_sha256" "6001777ea447cce3ae7f8cc6ab0ea89cda6289dae88ba9083f4a5a10f86ed8de"} {"question" "q-41787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ba79cff155e0b4bec660a0e94848c57d818a7735eb402933d36910253133e00d"} {"question" "q-41787" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/state.json" "file_sha256" "6001777ea447cce3ae7f8cc6ab0ea89cda6289dae88ba9083f4a5a10f86ed8de"} {"question" "q-41787" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-5,-5)"] ["math" "(-5\\sqrt{2},-5)"] ["math" "(-5\\sqrt{3},-5)"] ["math" "(-5\\sqrt{3},-5\\sqrt{2})"] ["math" "(-5\\sqrt{3},-5\\sqrt{3})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/state.json" "file_sha256" "6001777ea447cce3ae7f8cc6ab0ea89cda6289dae88ba9083f4a5a10f86ed8de"} {"question" "q-41787" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-5\\sqrt{3},-5)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/state.json" "file_sha256" "6001777ea447cce3ae7f8cc6ab0ea89cda6289dae88ba9083f4a5a10f86ed8de"} {"question" "q-87135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/assets/5507850932bb4061cd19cdbffa18aacf2b235293e643170ee78c4326a96e050d.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(9,-3\\sqrt{3})$, find the polar equation of the ray." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/state.json" "file_sha256" "6001777ea447cce3ae7f8cc6ab0ea89cda6289dae88ba9083f4a5a10f86ed8de"} {"question" "q-87135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-3\\sqrt{3}}{9}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we find the angle in the fourth quadrant that has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/state.json" "file_sha256" "6001777ea447cce3ae7f8cc6ab0ea89cda6289dae88ba9083f4a5a10f86ed8de"} {"question" "q-87135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ba79cff155e0b4bec660a0e94848c57d818a7735eb402933d36910253133e00d"} {"question" "q-87135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/state.json" "file_sha256" "6001777ea447cce3ae7f8cc6ab0ea89cda6289dae88ba9083f4a5a10f86ed8de"} {"question" "q-87135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}"] ["math" "\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}"] ["math" "\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}"] ["math" "\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/state.json" "file_sha256" "6001777ea447cce3ae7f8cc6ab0ea89cda6289dae88ba9083f4a5a10f86ed8de"} {"question" "q-87135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/state.json" "file_sha256" "6001777ea447cce3ae7f8cc6ab0ea89cda6289dae88ba9083f4a5a10f86ed8de"} {"question" "q-120712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/assets/2da1a7590ff9c8d403c040680c89f9bf310030d31399be7bd37209ef745a5bbd.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(4,-4)$, find the polar equation of the ray." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/state.json" "file_sha256" "6001777ea447cce3ae7f8cc6ab0ea89cda6289dae88ba9083f4a5a10f86ed8de"} {"question" "q-120712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-4}{4}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we find the angle in the fourth quadrant that has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/state.json" "file_sha256" "6001777ea447cce3ae7f8cc6ab0ea89cda6289dae88ba9083f4a5a10f86ed8de"} {"question" "q-120712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ba79cff155e0b4bec660a0e94848c57d818a7735eb402933d36910253133e00d"} {"question" "q-120712" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jak